<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahya ibn Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Yahya al-Mu'tali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahya ibn Ali al-Mu'tali was a Hammudid claimant to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, ruling there in 1021–1023 and 1025–1026. Driven from the capital, he made Málaga his base while still claiming the title of caliph, founding the Hammudid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Málaga, and was killed in 1035 fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ali.jpg" alt="Yahya ibn Ali" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_yahya_22332.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yahya was the son of Ali ibn Hammud, who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from the caliph Sulayman and was proclaimed caliph al-Nasir. Yahya then took his father's place as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>. When Ali was assassinated in 1018, Yahya's uncle al-Qasim claimed the throne. Yahya refused to recognise this succession, and in 1021 he marched on Córdoba with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> supporters. Al-Qasim fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and Yahya was proclaimed caliph al-Mu'tali on 13 August 1021.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His rule soon alienated his supporters, and fearing an overthrow he withdrew to his stronghold of Málaga in February 1023. Al-Qasim returned and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, but the people of the city, tired of turbulent Hammudid rule, rose after six months. Al-Qasim fled and hid in Jerez, where Yahya found him and brought him to Málaga; he was imprisoned and later assassinated.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> the short reigns of Abd al-Rahman V and Muhammad III followed. When Muhammad III fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1025, the city's aristocracy governed for six months and then asked Yahya to return. He arrived on 9 November 1025 to find an empty treasury and no working government. Showing little interest in ruling from the old capital, he left his vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ja">Abu Ja</a>'far ibn Musa in charge and went back to Málaga. The saqaliba, rivals of the pro-Berber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a>, then drove the Hammudid forces out of the city, and in June 1027 the aristocracy placed the Umayyad Hisham III on the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From Málaga Yahya kept the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, which his successors also claimed. With the support of the Zirids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> he took the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> and declared the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of Málaga. His years there were marked by heavy conflict with the Abbadid taifa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. He was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-i-al-mutayyad">Idris I al-Muta'ayyad</a>, and two of his sons, Hasan al-Mustansir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> al-Ali, later ruled Málaga as well.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yahya ibn Ali ibn Hammud al-Mu'tali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Driven from the capital, they made Málaga their base around 1026, when <b>Yahya ibn Ali al-Mutali</b> established his court there while still claiming the title of caliph.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-malaga">Taifa of Málaga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Carmona, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.47,-5.64&amp;year=1025" aria-label="Carmona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4409 815 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Yahya ibn Ali"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 1085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 1085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.47,-5.64&amp;year=1025">What was here in 1025? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
